Let $b$ be the number of blue marbles and $y$ be the number of yellow marbles. We have \begin{align*}
\frac{b}{y}&=\frac{4}{3} \\
\frac{b+5}{y-3}&=\frac{7}{3}
\end{align*}From the first equation, we have $b=\frac{4}{3}y$. Plugging this into the second equation, we get \begin{align*}
\frac{\frac{4}{3}y+5}{y-3}&=\frac{7}{3} \\
3\left(\frac{4}{3}y+5\right)&=7(y-3) \\
4y+15&=7y-21 \\
3y&=36 \\
y&=12
\end{align*}So there are $\frac{4}{3}\cdot12=\boxed{16}$ blue marbles. The answer is 16.

