To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we replace $f(x)$ with $x$ and solve for $x$. Thus, \begin{align*}
x&=a\cdot f^{-1}(x)+b \\
\Rightarrow\qquad x-b&=a\cdot f^{-1}(x) \\
\Rightarrow\qquad \frac{x-b}{a}&=f^{-1}(x)
\end{align*}We are given that $g(x)=2f^{-1}(x)$, so \begin{align*}
g(x)&=2f^{-1}(x) \\
3x+2&=2\cdot\frac{x-b}{a} \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 3ax+2a&=2x-2b \\
\Rightarrow\qquad (3a-2)x&=-2a-2b \\
\Rightarrow\qquad x&=\frac{2a+2b}{3a-2}
\end{align*}Since $g(x)=3x+2$, we have \begin{align*}
\frac{2a+2b}{3a-2}&=3 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 2a+2b&=9a-6 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 7a-2b&=6 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad \frac{a+b}{2}&=\frac{6}{7}
\end{align*}Therefore, $\dfrac{a+b}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{6}{7}}$. The answer is \dfrac{6}{7}.

