To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we replace $f(x)$ with $x$ and solve for $x$. Thus, \begin{align*}
x&=a\cdot f^{-1}(x)+b \\
\Rightarrow \quad x-b&=a\cdot f^{-1}(x) \\
\Rightarrow \quad \dfrac{x-b}{a}&=f^{-1}(x)
\end{align*}We are given that $g(x)=2f^{-1}(x)$, so $3x+2=2\cdot\dfrac{x-b}{a}$. Expanding and rearranging, we have \begin{align*}
3ax+2a&=2x-2b \\
\Rightarrow \quad (3a-2)x&=-2a-2b \\
\Rightarrow \quad x&=\dfrac{-2a-2b}{3a-2}
\end{align*}Since $g(x)=3x+2$, we have $\dfrac{-2a-2b}{3a-2}=3$. Solving, we have \begin{align*}
\dfrac{-2a-2b}{3a-2}&=3 \\
\Rightarrow \quad -2a-2b&=9a-6 \\
\Rightarrow \quad 11a-2b&=6 \\
\Rightarrow \quad \dfrac{a+b}{2}&=\dfrac{6}{11}
\end{align*}Thus, $\dfrac{a+b}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{6}{11}}$.The answer is: \frac{6}{11}

