Let $b$ be the number of blue marbles and $y$ be the number of yellow marbles. We have \begin{align*}
\frac{b}{y}&=\frac{4}{3} \\
\frac{b+5}{y-3}&=\frac{7}{3}
\end{align*}From the first equation, we have $b=\frac{4}{3}y$. Plugging this into the second equation, we get \begin{align*}
\frac{\frac{4}{3}y+5}{y-3}&=\frac{7}{3} \\
3\left(\frac{4}{3}y+5\right)&=7(y-3) \\
4y+15&=7y-21 \\
3y&=36 \\
y&=12
\end{align*}So there are $12\cdot\frac{4}{3}=\boxed{16}$ blue marbles before any were added. The answer is 16.

We can write
\begin{align*}
\frac{\cos x}{1 - \sin x} - \frac{\cos x}{1 + \sin x} &= \frac{\cos x (1 + \sin x)}{(1 - \sin x)(1 + \sin x)} - \frac{\cos x (1 - \sin x)}{(1 + \sin x)(1 - \sin x)} \\
&= \frac{\cos x (1 + \sin x)}{1 - \sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos x (1 - \sin x)}{1 - \sin^2 x} \\
&= \frac{\cos x (1 + \sin x)}{\cos^2 x} - \frac{\cos x (1 - \sin x)}{\cos^2 x} \\
&= \frac{1 + \sin x}{\cos x} - \frac{1 - \sin x}{\cos x} \\
&= \frac{2 \sin x}{\cos x} \\
&= \boxed{2 \tan x}.
\end{align*}The answer is: 2\tanx[/latex].
