\frac{b}{y}&=\frac{4}{3} \\
\frac{b+5}{y-3}&=\frac{7}{3}
\end{align*}Cross-multiplying the first equation gives $b=\frac{4}{3}y$. Cross-multiplying the second equation gives $3(b+5)=7(y-3)$. Substituting the first equation into the second gives $3\left(\frac{4}{3}y+5\right)=7(y-3)$. Solving for $y$ gives $y=12$. Substituting this into the first equation gives $b=\frac{4}{3}\cdot12=16$. Therefore, there were $\boxed{16}$ blue marbles in the bag before I added more.

