To subtract fractions, we need a common denominator. In this case, the least common multiple of 3 and 9 is 9. So, we rewrite the fractions with the common denominator of 9: \begin{align*}
\frac{1}{3} - \frac{2}{9} &= \frac{1 \cdot 3}{3 \cdot 3} - \frac{2 \cdot 1}{9 \cdot 1} \\
&= \frac{3}{9} - \frac{2}{9} \\
&= \frac{3-2}{9} \\
&= \frac{1}{9}.
\end{align*}Therefore, $\frac{1}{3} - \frac{2}{9} = \boxed{\frac{1}{9}}$.The answer is: \frac{1}{9}

