Using the identity $\sin \theta = \sin (\pi - \theta)$, we can rewrite the expression as
\[
\sin \frac{\pi}{12} + \sin \frac{11\pi}{12} + \sin \frac{3\pi}{12} + \sin \frac{9\pi}{12} + \sin \frac{5\pi}{12} + \sin \frac{7\pi}{12}.
\]
By the sum-to-product formula,
\begin{align*}
\sin \frac{\pi}{12} + \sin \frac{11\pi}{12} &= 2 \sin \frac{\pi}{3} \cos \frac{5\pi}{12} = \sqrt{3} \cos \frac{5\pi}{12}, \\
\sin \frac{3\pi}{12} + \sin \frac{9\pi}{12} &= 2 \sin \frac{\pi}{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{4} = \sqrt{2}, \\
\sin \frac{5\pi}{12} + \sin \frac{7\pi}{12} &= 2 \sin \frac{\pi}{2} \cos \frac{\pi}{12} = 2 \cos \frac{\pi}{12}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, the sum is
\[
\sqrt{3} \cos \frac{5\pi}{12} + \sqrt{2} + 2 \cos \frac{\pi}{12}.
\]
Since $\cos \frac{5\pi}{12} = \cos \frac{\pi}{12}$, this simplifies to $2 \cos \frac{\pi}{12} + \sqrt{2}$.

