As before, we can use synthetic division to divide $g(x)$ by $f(x)$ to find the third root.
$$\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-2x} & +3x & -2x & +x & -12 \\
\cline{2-6}
-x^2 & +3x & +4 & & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-2x^3} & +6x^2 & & & \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & -3x^2 & -2x & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & -3x^2 & +9x & & \\
\cline{3-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & 7x & +x & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 7x & -28 & \\
\cline{4-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0 & 29 & -12 \\
\end{array}$$Thus, we have $g(x) = -(x-3)(x^2-2x+4) + 29x - 12$. Since every root of $f(x)$ is also a root of $g(x)$, we know that $x^2-2x+4$ is divisible by $f(x)$. Therefore, the roots of $x^2-2x+4$ are the other two roots of $g(x)$. Using the quadratic formula, we have \begin{align*}
x &= \frac{2 \pm \sqrt{2^2-4(1)(4)}}{2(1)} \\
&= \frac{2 \pm \sqrt{-12}}{2} \\
&= \frac{2 \pm 2i\sqrt{3}}{2} \\
&= 1 \pm i\sqrt{3}.
\end{align*}Therefore, the third root of $g(x)$ is $\boxed{1 + i\sqrt{3}}$.The answer is: 1+i\sqrt{3}

