Square both sides of the equation $r = \cos \theta + \sin \theta.$  We get
\[r^2 = \cos^2 \theta + 2 \sin \theta \cos \theta + \sin^2 \theta.\]Then $r^2 = 1 + \sin 2 \theta,$ so $r^2 - 1 = \sin 2 \theta.$  Since $-1 \le \sin 2 \theta \le 1,$ we have $-2 \le r^2 - 1 \le 2,$ so $0 \le r \le \sqrt{3}.$  Therefore, the graph is a portion of the curve $r^2 - 1 = \sin 2 \theta,$ which is a line.  The answer is $\boxed{\text{(A)}}.$
