Let $b$ be the number of blue marbles and $y$ be the number of yellow marbles. We have the equations \begin{align*}
\frac{b}{y}&=\frac{4}{3} \\
\frac{b+5}{y-3}&=\frac{7}{3}
\end{align*}Cross-multiplying the first equation gives $b=\frac{4}{3}y$. Cross-multiplying the second equation gives $3(b+5)=7(y-3)$. Substituting the first equation into the second gives $3\left(\frac{4}{3}y+5\right)=7(y-3)$. Solving for $y$, we have \begin{align*}
4y+15&=7y-21 \\
3y&=36 \\
y&=12
\end{align*}Substituting this value into the first equation gives $b=\frac{4}{3}\cdot12=16$. So, there were $\boxed{16}$ blue marbles in the bag before I added more. The answer is 16.

