Since every root of $f(x)$ is also a root of $g(x)$, the polynomial $g(x)-f(x)$ has the same roots as $g(x)$ and $f(x)$. So, let's try to compute $g(x)-f(x)$. We have \begin{align*}
g(x)-f(x)&=(-2x^3+9x^2-x-12)-(-x^2+3x+4) \\
&=-2x^3+10x^2-4x-16 \\
&=-2(x^3-5x^2+2x+8) \\
&=-2(x-2)(x^2-3x-4).
\end{align*}By the quadratic formula, the roots of $x^2-3x-4$ are $x=\frac{3\pm\sqrt{25}}{2}=3\pm\sqrt{25}$. Since $3+\sqrt{25}$ is not a root of $f(x)$, it must be the third root of $g(x)$. So, the answer is $\boxed{3+\sqrt{25}}$.
