Consider the quadratic $f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c.$  Since $|f(x)| \le 1$ for $0 \le x \le 1,$ $f(x)$ lies below or on the $x$-axis on the interval $0 \le x \le 1.$  Thus, $f(x)$ has at most one real root.

Furthermore, if $f(x)$ has one real root, then this root must satisfy $x < 0$ or $x > 1,$ so $f(0)$ and $f(1)$ have the same sign.  Hence, $f(0)$ and $f(1)$ cannot both be negative.

If $f(0)$ and $f(1)$ have the same sign, then $f(x)$ is non-positive for all $0 \le x \le 1,$ so $a = b = c = 0.$  Then $|a| + |b| + |c| = 0.$

Otherwise, $f(0)$ is positive and $f(1)$ is negative, so $f(0) > 0 > f(1).$  Since $f(0)$ is positive, $c > 0.$  Furthermore, $f(1) < 0,$ so $a + b + c < 0.$  Since $c > 0,$ $a + b < -c.$  Also, $f(x)$ has a root at $x = 1,$ so $a + b + c = 0,$ which means $a + b = -c.$  Hence,
\[|a| + |b| + |c| = a + b + c = -(a + b) = c.\]Therefore, the maximum value of $|a| + |b| + |c|$ is $\boxed{1},$ which is achieved by $a = 0,$ $b = -1,$ and $c = 1.$The answer is: 1

