We solve the system of equations by first multiplying the second equation by 2, so that we can easily eliminate $y$. We have the system of equations \begin{align*}
2ax+2y&=b,\\
10x+2y&=-6.
\end{align*}Subtracting the first equation from the second, we get $10x-2ax=-6-b$, so $(10-2a)x=-6-b$. For this equation to have infinitely many solutions, the coefficient of $x$ must be 0, so $10-2a=0\Rightarrow a=5$. Substituting this into the equation, we get $(10-2\cdot5)x=-6-b\Rightarrow0x=-6-b\Rightarrow b=-6$. Thus, the ordered pair is $\boxed{(5,-6)}$.The answer is: (5,-6)
