Since $z^2 = 156 + 65i,$ $z^2 = 13 \cdot 12 + 13 \cdot 5i,$ so $z = \pm \sqrt{13} \sqrt{12 + 5i}.$  We can write $12 + 5i$ in the form $|z|(\cos \theta + i \sin \theta),$ where $|z|$ is the magnitude of $z$ and $\theta$ is the argument of $z.$  We have
\begin{align*}
|z| &= \sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} \\
&= 13.
\end{align*}Also, $\cos \theta = \frac{12}{13}$ and $\sin \theta = \frac{5}{13},$ so
\begin{align*}
\tan \theta &= \frac{\sin \theta}{\cos \theta} \\
&= \frac{5}{12}.
\end{align*}Therefore, $z = \pm \sqrt{13} \cdot 13^{1/2} (\cos \theta + i \sin \theta) = \pm 13 \sqrt{13} (\cos \theta + i \sin \theta),$ so $|z| = \boxed{13 \sqrt{13}}.$The answer is: 13\sqrt{13}

